WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.325
Twenty miles offshore, you are the ambulance.

2
00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:06.000
Help may be an hour away or more.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:11.975
So first aid isn’t optional for a charter captain, and your license requires the training.

4
00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:14.725
Here are the priorities, in order.

5
00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:17.100
Make sure the scene is safe for you.

6
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.600
Then check: does the person respond, and are they breathing normally?

7
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.625
Call for help early, on channel sixteen or by phone.

8
00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:30.800
The Coast Guard can put a doctor on the radio and arrange an evacuation.

9
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.500
Don’t wait to see if it gets better.

10
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.050
If they aren’t breathing normally, start CPR.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:45.100
Push hard and fast in the center of the chest: one hundred to one hundred twenty compressions a minute, about two inches deep.

12
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.200
How fast is that?

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.025
About two a second.

14
00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:52.700
Faster than most people expect.

15
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.525
Use an AED if you carry one.

16
00:00:55.775 --> 00:00:58.350
And keep your hands-on training current.

17
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.225
You can’t learn CPR from a slide.
